@1. What GP.BASIC is
.
=1. WHAT GP.BASIC IS
.
.Core keywords. 29 of them, compiled to p-code and handled by assembly in the
.runtime:
.
.GP.DO GP.LOOP GP.EXITDO GP.IF GP.ELSEIF GP.ELSE GP.ENDIF GP.SELECT GP.CASE
.GP.OTHER GP.ENDSEL GP.INSTR GP.COMP GP.STRPTR GP.ARRPTR GP.BOX GP.FILL
.GP.PRINTAT GP.CALL GP.A GP.X GP.Y GP.C GP.FN GP.BANKEDSTR GP.ENDBANKEDSTR
.GP.BSTR GP.BANKED GP.ENDBANKED
.
.They are assembly because BASIC is slow at per-character string scans,
.screen fills and VERA writes.
.
.The handlers occupy GPBase $2F00 to ObjectBase $3500: 1,536 bytes, page
.aligned, all or nothing. ScanGPUsage walks the finished p-code and drops the
.whole block from the object if nothing in it is reached.
.
.The block costs those 1,536 bytes. Maximum p-code is 20,992 bytes with 
.the block and 22,528 without: ObjectBase or GPBase to $9F00, less the
.2K frame stack and the 4K minimum workspace.
.
.Composites. GP.ASM, GP.ENDASM, GP.CHAR, GP.CONTAINS, GP.ISEMPTY, GP.HIBYTE,
.GP.LOBYTE, GP.BSTRCOUNT, GP.DEFPROC, GP.SUB. The compiler expands each into
.opcodes that already exist.
.
.The library. GPC-BASIC/: 17 .INC.BL modules, 25 .EXP.BL examples. Ordinary
.BASL, #INCLUDEd by path, called with GOSUB. Zero runtime bytes -- a module
.costs its own p-code, in the programs that include it. A program that has
.run out of low memory can put a module's #INCLUDE inside a GP.BANKED region
.and run it from a RAM bank, with no change to its callers; SEC 3.12 is where
.that is explained. Menus vertical and bar, panels, themes, entry fields,
.in-place case, trim and splice, shell sort, a screen rectangle to a RAM bank
.or a file, BMX into VERA.
.
.STASH.INC.BL, SORT.INC.BL, STRCASE.INC.BL and STRINGS.INC.BL are GP.ASM and
.still modules: as keywords their bytes would sit in the block, paid by every
.GP program. GP.ARRPTR and GP.STRPTR are what lets them out -- a BASL
.subroutine takes an address, not an array or a string.
.
.The division is assembly for loops and bulk moves, BASIC for everything
.else. LINEINPUT.GET waits on the keyboard, so speed does not apply to it;
.writing it in BASL saved 166 runtime bytes.
.
=SEE ALSO
.
>52|3.12 Code in a bank
>91|STASH.INC.BL -- save a text rectangle, and put it back.
>70|4.7 SORT.INC.BL -- shell sort a string array
>71|4.8 STRCASE.INC.BL -- case, in place
>62|4.2 STRINGS.INC.BL -- string helpers
